    </table>
   
    <h2>The Trap: The Dopamine Deficit</h2>
    <p>Despite their differences, both roads lead to the same destination: <strong>Tolerance.</strong></p>
   
    <p>The brain loves homeostasis (balance). If you constantly flood it with dopamine—whether through a chemical like nicotine or a super-stimulus like an algorithm—the brain will eventually fight back. It downregulates (removes) dopamine receptors to protect itself.</p>
   
    <p>This leads to a <strong>Dopamine Deficit State</strong>.<br>
    Eventually, the smoker doesn't smoke to feel good; they smoke just to feel <em>normal</em>. Similarly, the chronic scroller doesn't enjoy the videos anymore; they scroll because stopping makes them feel bored, anxious, and "under-stimulated."</p>
   
    <h2>Conclusion</h2>
    <p>One cigarette is a larger biological event than one TikTok video. However, the algorithmic loop is often more insidious because it is limitless. There is no "end" to the feed, and the rapid-fire nature of the rewards can exhaust your dopamine system just as effectively as a chemical stimulant.</p>
   
    <p>Understanding this mechanism is the first step to reclaiming your attention. Whether you are putting down the pack or putting down the phone, you are giving your brain the chance to reset its baseline and find joy in the ordinary again.</p>

